WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.810 line:88%
Hello. I’m Ari, your virtual toolkit guide.

2
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.180 line:88%
A working student may move between the roles of

3
00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:12.210 line:88%
employee, learner, parent,
caregiver, partner, or family member.

4
00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:15.500 line:88%
Roles differ from person to person,
but they often compete

5
00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:19.160 line:88%
for the same limited time,
energy, and attention.

6
00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:24.160 line:88%
When these demands build up,
a person may experience role overload.

7
00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:27.730 line:88%
Commitments may overlap, rest may be postponed,

8
00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:31.660 line:88%
boundaries can be hard to voice,
and the most urgent demand may take over.

9
00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:33.070 line:88%
This matters.

10
00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:37.320 line:88%
When responsibilities remain scattered,
conflicts may be noticed too late.

11
00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:40.250 line:88%
Rest and personal needs can be overlooked,

12
00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:44.430 line:88%
important conversations may be delayed,
and decisions may become reactive.

13
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.220 line:88%
The answer is not always to work harder.

14
00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:50.290 line:88%
It may start with knowing
which roles create pressure,

15
00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:54.390 line:88%
which commitments cannot move,
and which values should guide the decision.

16
00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:57.710 line:88%
This issue led Vanessa R. A. Salonoy to develop

17
00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:04.980 line:88%
the Work–Study–Life Balance Toolkit
for MO-SS031 – Understanding the Self.

18
00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:08.450 line:88%
She wanted to create more than
a calendar or a checklist.

19
00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.460 line:88%
She envisioned one connected
space where working students could

20
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:14.520 line:88%
understand their roles, organize commitments,

21
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:18.110 line:88%
work through conflicts,
and choose a realistic next step.

22
00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:22.850 line:88%
A regular planner is useful
for recording tasks and schedules.

23
00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:24.740 line:88%
This toolkit adds reflection.

24
00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:27.790 line:88%
It asks which role each responsibility serves,

25
00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:31.810 line:88%
whether it is fixed or flexible,
and what conversation it may need.

26
00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:34.450 line:88%
Vanessa chose an interactive website

27
00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:37.920 line:88%
so that this reflection could
become a guided journey.

28
00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:42.670 line:88%
Visitors move through connected rooms
with Ari, complete interactive activities,

29
00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:45.810 line:88%
and can switch between 3D and standard views.

30
00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:50.760 line:88%
The six components work as one journey,
and each step prepares the next.

31
00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:53.190 line:88%
Role Mapping helps users identify their roles,

32
00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:57.250 line:88%
responsibilities, expectations,
and pressure points.

33
00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:00.200 line:88%
The Weekly Priority Planner and Calendar
turn that awareness

34
00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.240 line:88%
into a realistic Monday-to-Sunday plan,

35
00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:06.130 line:88%
guided by the user’s priorities and values.

36
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.560 line:88%
Each entry is marked fixed or flexible,

37
00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:11.930 line:88%
with room for rest, health,
travel, and buffer time.

38
00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:18.410 line:88%
When entries clash, the Schedule-Conflict Plan
helps users weigh their options.

39
00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.010 line:88%
The decision stays with them.

40
00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.840 line:88%
Communication Templates help users
prepare a respectful message,

41
00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.940 line:88%
without assuming it will be approved.

42
00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:28.710 line:88%
The Mental Well-Being Check-In
gives users a pause

43
00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:32.670 line:88%
to notice how they are doing
and whether the plan still fits.

44
00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:35.830 line:88%
It is not a diagnosis, not medical advice,

45
00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:38.500 line:88%
and not a replacement for professional support.

46
00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:40.990 line:88%
Finally, the Reflection Tracker and Action Plan

47
00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:44.290 line:88%
help users choose one manageable next step.

48
00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:47.870 line:88%
To use the toolkit, begin with Role Mapping

49
00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:52.000 line:88%
to identify the roles
and responsibilities creating pressure.

50
00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:56.440 line:88%
Continue to the Planning Room
to organize the week and identify conflicts.

51
00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:00.570 line:88%
Use the remaining rooms to consider
options, prepare conversations,

52
00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:04.860 line:88%
check personal capacity,
and record one realistic next step.

53
00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:07.190 line:88%
Progress stays in the same browser,

54
00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:10.160 line:88%
and the final Summary brings
the responses together.

55
00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:13.820 line:88%
Here is a fictional example.

56
00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.180 line:88%
A fixed Friday work shift may
overlap with the time needed

57
00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:19.800 line:88%
to complete an important research paper.

58
00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:22.190 line:88%
The calendar makes the pressure visible.

59
00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:26.010 line:88%
The conflict plan helps the student
consider realistic options.

60
00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:31.180 line:88%
A communication template provides a starting
point for a respectful conversation.

61
00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:34.120 line:88%
The toolkit does not send it or contact anyone,

62
00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:36.730 line:88%
and an accommodation is not guaranteed.

63
00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:40.660 line:88%
The check-in encourages the student
to consider personal capacity,

64
00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:44.280 line:88%
and the action plan records
the next manageable step.

65
00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:46.500 line:88%
The final decision is the student’s.

66
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:52.480 line:88%
Used this way, the toolkit can help
users recognize sources of pressure,

67
00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:56.810 line:88%
notice conflicts earlier, and prepare
for conversations about boundaries.

68
00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:02.450 line:88%
The intended impact is not to create
a perfect schedule or remove every conflict.

69
00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:06.380 line:88%
It is to help working students
understand their situation earlier,

70
00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:09.220 line:88%
make more conscious and realistic choices,

71
00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:12.830 line:88%
and respond to competing responsibilities
with greater clarity.

72
00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:16.630 line:88%
This project reflects Understanding the Self

73
00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:20.120 line:88%
because responsible planning
begins with self-awareness.

74
00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:25.690 line:88%
Users examine their roles, values,
limits, needs, patterns, and choices,

75
00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:28.720 line:88%
then connect that understanding
to practical decisions.

76
00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:32.730 line:88%
Balance does not always mean
giving every role equal time.

77
00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:35.360 line:88%
It means making deliberate
choices based on responsibilities,

78
00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:39.510 line:88%
values, priorities, and realistic capacity.

79
00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:41.850 line:88%
The Work–Study–Life Balance Toolkit

80
00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:45.370 line:88%
does not promise a life
without competing demands.

81
00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:49.060 line:88%
It provides a place to pause,
understand what is happening,

82
00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:52.790 line:88%
organize what matters,
and choose the next responsible step.

83
00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:56.100 line:88%
For working students carrying
several roles at once,

84
00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:59.130 line:88%
greater clarity can be
an important place to begin.

85
00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:01.820 line:88%
Start with Role Mapping whenever you are ready.

86
00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:03.610 line:88%
Thank you for watching.

87
00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:16.180 line:88%
This toolkit is for educational
and reflective use only.
